 ```
 
+### Export
+
+`GET /v1/export` streams every document of the user as newline-delimited JSON: one line per
+document, shaped like `GET /v1/documents/:collection/:id` plus `"type": "document"`, ordered by
+collection and id (the `documents` primary key), then `{"type":"end","documents":<count>}`. An
+export without that last line is incomplete: the server cuts one off when it fails midway or
+when it could send its client nothing for `EXPORT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS`. Pages are read in one
+REPEATABLE READ transaction, so the export is the state at the time of the request, and only as
+fast as the client takes the lines. Each running export holds a database connection; beyond
+`EXPORT_MAX_CONCURRENT` the answer is 503 `unavailable`.
+
 ## Layout
 
 ```
@@ -155,6 +167,8 @@ packages/*/test/            vitest suites; test/helpers have the app builders an
 | `PULL_MAX_LIMIT`, `PULL_DEFAULT_LIMIT` | `1000`, `200` | events per pull page |
 | `BODY_LIMIT_BYTES` | `33554432` | largest request body (a full push of the largest documents fits) |
 | `SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS` | `10000` | graceful shutdown limit |
+| `EXPORT_MAX_CONCURRENT` | `2` | exports running at once, at most `DATABASE_POOL_MAX` − 1 |
+| `EXPORT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS` | `30000` | an export that could send its client nothing for this long is cut off |
 
 The server refuses to start, listing every problem, when a variable is invalid. See
 `.env.example`. The command-line client reads `SYNC_SERVER_URL`, `SYNC_TOKEN`, `SYNC_DB`
@@ -184,8 +198,12 @@ npm run cli -- set notes groceries title=Groceries tags=home,weekly
 npm run cli -- sync
 npm run cli -- list notes
 npm run cli -- status
+npm run cli -- export documents.ndjson     # the server's documents, one JSON per line
 ```
 
+`export` writes `<file>.partial` and renames it to `<file>` once the server's `end` line has
+arrived and the count matches, so `<file>` only ever holds a complete export.
+
 It exits 0 on success, 1 on a failure, 2 on a usage error and 75 on a failure worth retrying
 later.
 
